(5528662, 794122, 62357, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>Clearly this image met the challenge perfectly.  You can see in their faces how happy they are to be together.  So, nothing wrong with regard to the challenge.<br/> <br/>I think the main reason this did not score higher, is because it feels like a well taken snapshot.  This is not necessarily a bad thing, a snapshot may very well be the most important photograph you ever take, but in terms of scoring high on DPC, then it is quite important.  This does not feel as though the composition was really planned.<br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club Clearly this image met the challenge perfectly.  You can see in their faces how happy they are to be together.  So, nothing wrong with regard to the challenge. I think the main reason this did not score higher, is because it feels like a well taken snapshot.  This is not necessarily a bad thing, a snapshot may very well be the most important photograph you ever take, but in terms of scoring high on DPC, then it is quite important.  This does not feel as though the composition was really planned. ', '2009-06-15 05:10:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(5528670, 796135, 62357, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>As far as meeting the challenge topic, I''m afraid this image is probably relying too much on the title.  That said, I do think it is an interesting and creative way of looking at this and certainly beats the kids and pets shots (in my not so humble opinion!) hands down.  But for DPC of course, I think it will hurt you a lot when the only link to the challenge is the title.<br/> <br/>Technically speaking, of course, the completely blown out sky hurts you a lot.  The composition also feels a bit too cramped.  Maybe if you had stood a bit further back and got more of the whole street in the shot? I loved that the colours of the walls matched the colours in the laundry.  <br/> <br/>Sorry it didn''t do better, but it was a quite brave image for this challenge.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club As far as meeting the challenge topic, I''m afraid this image is probably relying too much on the title.  That said, I do think it is an interesting and creative way of looking at this and certainly beats the kids and pets shots (in my not so humble opinion!) hands down.  But for DPC of course, I think it will hurt you a lot when the only link to the challenge is the title. Technically speaking, of course, the completely blown out sky hurts you a lot.  The composition also feels a bit too cramped.  Maybe if you had stood a bit further back and got more of the whole street in the shot? I loved that the colours of the walls matched the colours in the laundry.   Sorry it didn''t do better, but it was a quite brave image for this challenge.', '2009-06-15 05:24:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(5529120, 795946, 86090, '<td valign="top">**Greeting from the Critique Club**<br/> <br/>This is a cute idea for the challenge, showing friendship between humans and animals, however because of the space it takes, seems to be more about your finger than anything else. IMO getting closer would have give more detail on the laddy bug, giving more impact to you picture, and because of her pposition in relation to the sun, I can see some shadows that make lose some detail on her face.<br/> <br/>DPC voters like cleaner backgrounds and I think the blue on the left is a bit distracting, try to be aware of you bg whenever you are taking pictures, working with a different apperture will also help you blur out your bg a bit more.<br/> <br/>I hope this helps, and we are looking forward to seeing more of your work on the next challenges, look in to the side challenges too, you get a lot of feedback and you can ask specific questions you might have about your pictures.</td>', '**Greeting from the Critique Club** This is a cute idea for the challenge, showing friendship between humans and animals, however because of the space it takes, seems to be more about your finger than anything else. IMO getting closer would have give more detail on the laddy bug, giving more impact to you picture, and because of her pposition in relation to the sun, I can see some shadows that make lose some detail on her face. DPC voters like cleaner backgrounds and I think the blue on the left is a bit distracting, try to be aware of you bg whenever you are taking pictures, working with a different apperture will also help you blur out your bg a bit more. I hope this helps, and we are looking forward to seeing more of your work on the next challenges, look in to the side challenges too, you get a lot of feedback and you can ask specific questions you might have about your pictures.', '2009-06-15 11:49:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(5529192, 795637, 30982, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>I''d love to tell you all the stuff that''s wrong with this picture, but really, there ain''t much wrong with it at all.  It fits the challenge, it''s heartwarming, both models have excellent expressions and are well lit, AND you have one heckuva nice backdrop here.  Your commenters really liked it, the score and placing are good as well.  About the only thing I could suggest would be to crop out the posts left and right.  They do provide nice natural framing, but the fact they aren''t straight (and there''s that wee bit of space beyond the one on the left) create a slight imbalance.  Lovely photograph!</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! I''d love to tell you all the stuff that''s wrong with this picture, but really, there ain''t much wrong with it at all.  It fits the challenge, it''s heartwarming, both models have excellent expressions and are well lit, AND you have one heckuva nice backdrop here.  Your commenters really liked it, the score and placing are good as well.  About the only thing I could suggest would be to crop out the posts left and right.  They do provide nice natural framing, but the fact they aren''t straight (and there''s that wee bit of space beyond the one on the left) create a slight imbalance.  Lovely photograph!', '2009-06-15 12:22:05.000000', NULL, 0),
(5529237, 795996, 30982, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>I think this one was probably a little bit title-dependent to some voters - we could assume they were friends, but the picture doesn''t convey that super-clearly (and DPC loves super-clear challenge tie-ins!)  You mentioned that you didn''t do any photoshopping - I went looking through your portfolio and some of the other shots from this photo shoot are considerably better in presentation.  You''ve got the basics down well in this one - good light, very good with the makeup and such, and good poses (though I''m not entirely sure of the relevance of the cigarette lighting part) but it does need to be leveled, curved, sharpened at a minimum to bring out the picture.  I can tell from your other work that you know what you''re doing with Photoshop, so I assume you may be unsure of what is and isn''t permitted in Basic editing?  Anything which is applied overall to the photo (aforementioned levels, curves, brightness/contrast) etc is fine as long as it''s applied uniformly (no spot editing, no masks) and in normal blending mode.  Make sense?  It definitely appears you guys had a great time!  Feel free to PM me if you have any questions.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! I think this one was probably a little bit title-dependent to some voters - we could assume they were friends, but the picture doesn''t convey that super-clearly (and DPC loves super-clear challenge tie-ins!)  You mentioned that you didn''t do any photoshopping - I went looking through your portfolio and some of the other shots from this photo shoot are considerably better in presentation.  You''ve got the basics down well in this one - good light, very good with the makeup and such, and good poses (though I''m not entirely sure of the relevance of the cigarette lighting part) but it does need to be leveled, curved, sharpened at a minimum to bring out the picture.  I can tell from your other work that you know what you''re doing with Photoshop, so I assume you may be unsure of what is and isn''t permitted in Basic editing?  Anything which is applied overall to the photo (aforementioned levels, curves, brightness/contrast) etc is fine as long as it''s applied uniformly (no spot editing, no masks) and in normal blending mode.  Make sense?  It definitely appears you guys had a great time!  Feel free to PM me if you have any questions.', '2009-06-15 12:40:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(5529452, 795559, 2170, '<td valign="top">greetings from the critique club!<br/> <br/>well, judging from the ribbon there''s not much to critique here.  technically, it''s an excellent photo with great lines, color, and composition.  my only semi-negative thought about it is that i can see why some members would not think it appropriate for a "friends" challenge.<br/> <br/>i think if the female''s foot had popped up ever so slightly above the horizon that it would be more noticeable.  otherwise, however, i think the pose is excellent.<br/> <br/>overall an amazing capture.  excellent work!<br/> <br/>rob</td>', 'greetings from the critique club! well, judging from the ribbon there''s not much to critique here.  technically, it''s an excellent photo with great lines, color, and composition.  my only semi-negative thought about it is that i can see why some members would not think it appropriate for a "friends" challenge. i think if the female''s foot had popped up ever so slightly above the horizon that it would be more noticeable.  otherwise, however, i think the pose is excellent. overall an amazing capture.  excellent work! rob', '2009-06-15 14:11:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(5529467, 795894, 2170, '<td valign="top">greetings from the critique club!<br/> <br/>i think this is a great idea for the challenge.  as to why it didn''t score very well, i''m going to guess two things: lighting and composition.<br/> <br/>a photographer friend of mine told me once that the absolute worst thing you can do in an image is unintentionally cut off someone in the shot.  like, not capturing the feet of a person standing, etc.  obviously you can violate that rule for dramatic effect, but in a situation like this it seems that the half dog in the foreground of the shot is pretty distracting and takes away a lot from the photo.  had he been smaller or more in the background it might not have mattered so much.<br/> <br/>the concept is great, and the action is wonderful to capture.  i don''t know if you considered it or not, but if your camera has a burst mode you could use that to get a lot of action shots and then delete those that don''t work out.  the jumping and the ears of the dogs on the right work extremely well for this challenge -- your best bet would have been to put the focus of the image on them.<br/> <br/>finally, the bright sunlight washes out a lot of the detail.  while it''s not always avoidable, i''d suggest working on bringing out the colors of the photo more in order to give it more impact.<br/> <br/>good luck!<br/>rob<br/></td>', 'greetings from the critique club! i think this is a great idea for the challenge.  as to why it didn''t score very well, i''m going to guess two things: lighting and composition. a photographer friend of mine told me once that the absolute worst thing you can do in an image is unintentionally cut off someone in the shot.  like, not capturing the feet of a person standing, etc.  obviously you can violate that rule for dramatic effect, but in a situation like this it seems that the half dog in the foreground of the shot is pretty distracting and takes away a lot from the photo.  had he been smaller or more in the background it might not have mattered so much. the concept is great, and the action is wonderful to capture.  i don''t know if you considered it or not, but if your camera has a burst mode you could use that to get a lot of action shots and then delete those that don''t work out.  the jumping and the ears of the dogs on the right work extremely well for this challenge -- your best bet would have been to put the focus of the image on them. finally, the bright sunlight washes out a lot of the detail.  while it''s not always avoidable, i''d suggest working on bringing out the colors of the photo more in order to give it more impact. good luck!rob', '2009-06-15 14:19:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(5529475, 794916, 2170, '<td valign="top">greetings from the critique club!<br/> <br/>overall, i think this is a great idea for the shot.  i''m not sure if you meant anything by having the empty chair there, but it almost feels like a "missing man" formation kind of thing.<br/> <br/>i think the thing that is most hurting this image (compared to the 2nd place winner) is that the sunset/sky is so dark that it doesn''t really make the silhouettes pop.  at first glance, there''s nothing that really grabs your attention and draws you into the photograph.  you see it once you look at the image for a while, but there''s not much instant impact.<br/> <br/>it''s a good concept and fairly well-done.  i just think it''s suffering from a lack of "BAM!"<br/> <br/>rob</td>', 'greetings from the critique club! overall, i think this is a great idea for the shot.  i''m not sure if you meant anything by having the empty chair there, but it almost feels like a "missing man" formation kind of thing. i think the thing that is most hurting this image (compared to the 2nd place winner) is that the sunset/sky is so dark that it doesn''t really make the silhouettes pop.  at first glance, there''s nothing that really grabs your attention and draws you into the photograph.  you see it once you look at the image for a while, but there''s not much instant impact. it''s a good concept and fairly well-done.  i just think it''s suffering from a lack of "BAM!" rob', '2009-06-15 14:24:28.000000', NULL, 0),
(5529489, 794047, 2170, '<td valign="top">greetings from the critique club!<br/> <br/>i''m a huge fan of this kind of "slice of life" photography, so this photo immediately does it for me.  what killed your score, my guess, is the fact that people didn''t see how it fit into the challenge.<br/> <br/>because the two individuals are so blended into the background, it''s easy to miss the 2nd person (if not just the first).  therefore, most voters probably didn''t get the "friends" connection.<br/> <br/>otherwise, i think the lighting and composition are great, and i love the black and white coloring.  very timeless, very cool.<br/> <br/>rob<br/></td>', 'greetings from the critique club! i''m a huge fan of this kind of "slice of life" photography, so this photo immediately does it for me.  what killed your score, my guess, is the fact that people didn''t see how it fit into the challenge. because the two individuals are so blended into the background, it''s easy to miss the 2nd person (if not just the first).  therefore, most voters probably didn''t get the "friends" connection. otherwise, i think the lighting and composition are great, and i love the black and white coloring.  very timeless, very cool. rob', '2009-06-15 14:29:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(5529494, 795816, 2170, '<td valign="top">greetings from the critique club!<br/> <br/>well, judging from the score and your comments i don''t know how much there is to say about this.  i think it''s a darling photo and very well-done (for whatever you were using it for).  the emotion and poses are perfect, as are the costuming.  there''s definitely a story here and it really draws you in.<br/> <br/>the edited version you posted takes care of most of my nitpicks, so there''s no need to mention them.  overall, an excellent shot!<br/> <br/>rob</td>', 'greetings from the critique club! well, judging from the score and your comments i don''t know how much there is to say about this.  i think it''s a darling photo and very well-done (for whatever you were using it for).  the emotion and poses are perfect, as are the costuming.  there''s definitely a story here and it really draws you in. the edited version you posted takes care of most of my nitpicks, so there''s no need to mention them.  overall, an excellent shot! rob', '2009-06-15 14:31:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(5529504, 795678, 2170, '<td valign="top">greetings from the critique club!<br/> <br/>while i appreciate the capture in the moment, i think the most distracting part of this image is the crashing wave in the background.  it really takes away from what is going on in the foreground.<br/> <br/>the title is funny and apt.<br/> <br/>i think if this had been shot from a different angle so there wasn''t such a busy background it would have had more impact for the viewer.<br/> <br/>good luck!<br/>rob<br/></td>', 'greetings from the critique club! while i appreciate the capture in the moment, i think the most distracting part of this image is the crashing wave in the background.  it really takes away from what is going on in the foreground. the title is funny and apt. i think if this had been shot from a different angle so there wasn''t such a busy background it would have had more impact for the viewer. good luck!rob', '2009-06-15 14:34:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(5529556, 796073, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!  I wondered if I should pass on this shot and see if it ended up in Jelena''s lap <img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=62787" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">purpleflutterby13</a>, she is very fond of lemurs. :-)<br/> <br/>Initial impression: Love the expression of startlement on the lemurs'' faces, very cute.<br/> <br/>Technical: Composition is a little vague, but unless a handler is nearby to help out a little, zoo animals generally don''t take direction very well.  The shadows are a little harsh, could''ve been dealt with in post-processing.  I do agree that the dof could be a little deeper, maybe another stop or two.  <br/> <br/>Artistic: Definitely a family shot, and no doubt you''re shooting at a good distance.  I also have the D40 and that kit lens; glad that you were able to get quite a pleasing shot with it.  Pity the lemurs on the left weren''t on the right and vice-versa!  However the slightly haphazard comp and air of distraction about the lemurs just drives home that they are wild animals. You finished in the top 50% so well done.<br/> <br/>FWIW: Overall, a very sweet picture and a good one considering the subject matter. Look forward to seeing more entries from you!<br/> <br/>Feel free to PM me with any questions,<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  I wondered if I should pass on this shot and see if it ended up in Jelena''s lap  purpleflutterby13, she is very fond of lemurs. :-) Initial impression: Love the expression of startlement on the lemurs'' faces, very cute. Technical: Composition is a little vague, but unless a handler is nearby to help out a little, zoo animals generally don''t take direction very well.  The shadows are a little harsh, could''ve been dealt with in post-processing.  I do agree that the dof could be a little deeper, maybe another stop or two.   Artistic: Definitely a family shot, and no doubt you''re shooting at a good distance.  I also have the D40 and that kit lens; glad that you were able to get quite a pleasing shot with it.  Pity the lemurs on the left weren''t on the right and vice-versa!  However the slightly haphazard comp and air of distraction about the lemurs just drives home that they are wild animals. You finished in the top 50% so well done. FWIW: Overall, a very sweet picture and a good one considering the subject matter. Look forward to seeing more entries from you! Feel free to PM me with any questions, Susan', '2009-06-15 14:55:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(5530030, 796241, 37845, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>First, I would like to say that I like the photo. I think that the focus and sharpness is good, and I like the square crop. Some things that would improve a photo like this would be if you captured it with its eyes open, and had a better background. <br/> <br/>While you didnt receive many comments, one of them did allude to the fact that they thought it was a shoehorn, and didnt relate to the challenge. While I personally think it does, as I would assume that any pet would imply friendship, I also understand that not all voters will agree with this interpretation of the challenge. So, that being writ, I think your score suffered because of voters treating this as DNMC. <br/> <br/>But, also keep in mind that you got 3 votes of 10, so you struck a cord with some voters. <br/> <br/>VitaminB</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club First, I would like to say that I like the photo. I think that the focus and sharpness is good, and I like the square crop. Some things that would improve a photo like this would be if you captured it with its eyes open, and had a better background.  While you didnt receive many comments, one of them did allude to the fact that they thought it was a shoehorn, and didnt relate to the challenge. While I personally think it does, as I would assume that any pet would imply friendship, I also understand that not all voters will agree with this interpretation of the challenge. So, that being writ, I think your score suffered because of voters treating this as DNMC.  But, also keep in mind that you got 3 votes of 10, so you struck a cord with some voters.  VitaminB', '2009-06-15 18:13:49.000000', NULL, 0),
(5530036, 796117, 67258, '<td valign="top">I agree with Steve regarding the location of the shot - the car wasn''t the best of backdrops, and the cut off feet are a bit of a problem.  But that aside, you have a pleasing portrait of the dog, with a good tonal range.</td>', 'I agree with Steve regarding the location of the shot - the car wasn''t the best of backdrops, and the cut off feet are a bit of a problem.  But that aside, you have a pleasing portrait of the dog, with a good tonal range.', '2009-06-15 18:16:22.000000', NULL, 0),
